america's top diplomat anthony blinken  is in geneva to meet with russian  foreign minister sergey lavrov for make  or break talks that could determine war  or peace in europe  on thursday blinken was in berlin where  he held talks with german foreign  minister annalina bierbach and their  british and french counterparts a  meeting meant to show a united front and  clear up any misunderstanding about  western unity regarding russian  aggression against ukraine  the stakes are high  u.s secretary of state anthony blinken  landing in berlin to try and rally  america's allies and prevent a war  unity is the main goal here blinken and  his german french and british  counterparts discuss the intense fears  that russia may soon invade ukraine  their focus how to credibly deter moscow  with warnings of what could follow  even as we're relentless in pursuing  this diplomatic path  we'll continue to make very clear that  if moscow chooses the path of further  aggression  we will impose swift and massive costs  we urgently call on russia to take steps  towards the escalation  any further aggression would have  serious  but consequences by the us president a  day earlier have raised questions about  whether those consequences really do  apply to any act of aggression  russia will be held accountable  if it invades and it depends on what it  does it's one thing if it's a minor  incursion and then we end up having to  fight about what to do and not do etc  but if they actually do what they're  capable of doing with the force of mass  on the border it is going to be a  disaster for russia  the white house later tried to clarify  insisting that the west would react come  what may but the issue has highlighted  questions over what level of aggression  would trigger what kind of response the  us has made it clear that it would  expect berlin to target the  controversial nordstream 2 pipeline  which links germany to russian gas  it's also worth noting that gas is not  flowing through nordstrom 2 yet which  means that the pipeline is leveraged  for germany the united states and our  allies not russia  for now though there's another round of  diplomacy coming up anthony blinken will  meet his russian counterpart in geneva  on friday seen as one of the last  chances to find a way of avoiding war  and my colleague alexander farnam is in  geneva to follow those talks for us  if few ten stays there between the us  and russia what are we expecting from  from today's meeting  i would say that the expectations are  not very high here in geneva on this  very stormy windy day and you as  secretary of state lincoln told the  german press that he is not expecting  any breakthroughs that it's more about  taking stock of  where we stand right now and whether a  diplomatic solution to the crisis can be  found and what we have heard from the  washrooms they are also quite  pessimistic and they are continuing with  their very aggressive rhetoric  and there are demanding written  guarantees from the u.s and from nato  and u.s secretary of state lincoln has  already said that the u.s is so far not  prepared to give them a written response  lincoln has met with his eu counterparts  or at least some of his eu counterparts  this week do you think is there really a  united front towards russia  well i think that they are united in  what they are ready to do if there will  be a russian invasion of ukraine and  they have made clear that they will be  massive and severe consequences however  we also have to say and that is  something uh what in  the you asked president joe biden has  admitted  in a press conference that they are not  so united when we speak about to what  extent they are ready to impose  political and economic sanctions with  some european allies like germany or  france for example who have been in the  past quite reluctant of uh  of inflicting too much pain on russia  well russian troops uh have gathered  near the ukrainian border how have nato  allies 